\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation; slightly higher than per-generator macro average in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}). Identical XGBoost configuration. 7-feature: one 410M model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles: two models, 1.4B params (2{,}868\,MB). Individual detection scores (N/A) share the reference-model forward pass; per-method timing is not separately profiled.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 vs.\ inference cost (8-gen pooled). The 7-feature ensemble matches multi-detector ensembles at 2.4$\times$ lower cost; individual detectors vary widely when repurposed for depth.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

The 7-feature ensemble surpasses all individual baselines in 3-class F1 ($\Delta$\,=\,6.7--36.3\,pp; Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}) and the pure 4-detector ensemble in binary F1 (0.705 vs.\ 0.700).
Adding DDR's surface features to the four detectors yields the highest binary F1 (0.708), marginally above the 7-feature set at 2.4$\times$ the inference cost.
This result underscores that DDR's primary contribution is not classification performance---where multi-detector ensembles match or marginally exceed it---but the decomposition of depth signal into location-sensitive and shape-stable components, a structural distinction absent from both binary detection methods and model collapse analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

To assess whether depth signal extends beyond handcrafted features, we fine-tune a pretrained language model end-to-end as a depth classifier using QLoRA \cite{qlora}.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We fine-tune Qwen3-0.6B-Base with LoRA rank 16, learning rate $2 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 epochs, framing depth estimation as 5-class sequence classification (gen-0 through gen-4).\footnote{Evaluated on the original 6-generator subset; XGBoost 3-class F1\,=\,0.789. We expect 8-generator training to further improve given additional diversity; the comparison direction is preserved.}
For 3-class evaluation, we collapse predictions into gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+ post hoc.
All results use the same LOGO protocol as the main experiments.

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\small
\caption{QLoRA end-to-end depth classification vs.\ XGBoost on handcrafted features, under LOGO evaluation.}
\label{tab:qlora}
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{5-class F1} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Per-class F1 (5-class)} \\
\midrule
XGBoost (7 features) & 0.501 $\pm$ 0.038 & 0.789 & --- \\
QLoRA (Qwen3-0.6B)   & 0.583 $\pm$ 0.010 & 0.905 $\pm$ 0.003 & 0.999\,/\,0.796\,/\,0.413\,/\,0.259\,/\,0.447 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Results.}
QLoRA achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.003), a +11.6\,pp improvement over the XGBoost baseline (0.789).
On 5-class evaluation, QLoRA reaches F1\,=\,0.583 ($\pm$0.010) vs.\ 0.501 for XGBoost (+8.2\,pp), with the largest gains at gen-1 (0.796 vs.\ 0.634) and gen-2 (0.413 vs.\ 0.268).
The gen-2/gen-3 boundary remains the primary failure mode (gen-3 F1\,=\,0.259), consistent with the feature-level analysis in \S\ref{sec:discussion}.

\paragraph{Hyperparameter robustness.}
A second run with LoRA rank 32 and seed 123 yields 5-class F1\,=\,0.587 ($\pm$0.005) and 3-class F1\,=\,0.905 ($\pm$0.004), which confirms stability across hyperparameter and seed variation ($\Delta < 1$\,pp on both metrics).

\paragraph{Implications.}
The substantial 3-class improvement demonstrates that raw token embeddings contain depth signal beyond what summary statistics capture; the seven handcrafted features are a strong but lossy compression of the full distributional information.
End-to-end fine-tuning exploits token-level patterns (e.g., positional regularities, n-gram repetitions) that aggregate features cannot represent.
This confirms that the depth signal documented in our main analysis is real and exploitable beyond engineered features, while the handcrafted approach retains practical advantages: interpretability, computational efficiency (no GPU inference), and the ability to reveal the differential depth response pattern that motivates the task formulation.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Recursive generation leaves a systematic distributional signature: central-tendency features drift monotonically with depth while shape features remain an order of magnitude more stable---a dichotomy we formalize as differential depth response (DDR), validated by five independent effect-size metrics across eight generators from five model families.
This decomposition enables cross-generator depth classification (leak-free binary F1\,=\,0.694 under LOGO, 0.830 on cross-model chains) and provides an analytical lens complementary to model collapse: fixed-parameter sampling compresses distributional location while preserving shape, whereas retraining erodes both.
Current boundaries are instruction tuning---where alignment reshapes the generation attractor---and frontier scale, where preliminary 14B evidence confirms the DDR partition with attenuated effect sizes; 70B+ models remain untested.

